\f1\fs22 The knock at the door was timid\'97uncharacteristic, Sarah thought, for anyone who lived in proximity to Eloise Shakti\'92s compound. She opened the door cautiously, already expecting trouble, and found herself greeted by the distinctly awkward smile of Johnny Stoat. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Sarah, my dear! Looking radiant as ever," Johnny exclaimed with the enthusiasm of a door-to-door salesman with a quota to meet. He teetered in place, holding out something in one hand that appeared to be a gift bag, and in the other, what looked suspiciously like an orchid encased in cellophane.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Johnny," Sarah said flatly, already exhausted. "What a surprise."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I thought I\'92d come to check on you. You know, after the... stress of recent events." He stepped past her into the house without waiting for an invitation, a maneuver executed with the smug nonchalance of someone convinced his presence could only be delightful.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shut the door, took a deep breath, and turned. "You mean the whole elevator assassination attempt thing? Or the part where the Elvi informally canonized me as 'Holy Momma'?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Both valid causes for concern." Johnny nodded sagely, his eyes flitting briefly to her belly before quickly retreating skyward. "You\'92ve been through a lot. Women in your condition\'97uh, your unique condition, I mean\'97need to rest!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah arched an eyebrow. "Rest? You do know I work for Eloise Shakti, right? That\'92s not really part of the benefits package."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny let out a small laugh, clearly anticipating that his follow-up gesture would smooth over all concerns. "Well, you\'92ll be pleased to know I\'92ve come prepared." He thrust the gift bag toward her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah took it and pulled out its contents. "Stress balls?" she asked incredulously, holding up two squishy spheres emblazoned with corporate motivational slogans. "One of these says, \'91Crunch Those Deadlines!\'92 and the other says, \'91Stress Is For Losers.\'92 Seriously, Johnny?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"And there\'92s more!" Johnny piped up, visibly flustered but doubling down. He gestured to the orchid. "See? Something lovely and serene to brighten the place. It calms the mind, or so the lady at the garden center assured me."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I could really use something that calms you, Johnny," she muttered under her breath.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Nothing says concerned coworker like an executive stress ball, right?" he went on, oblivious to the razor edge creeping into her demeanor. "Plus, this isn\'92t just a social call. I wanted to share some ideas about your involvement in the Gyges project. You\'92ve been such a tremendous asset, but I worry\'97ahem\'97that perhaps it's taking a toll. Your health comes first, of course."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah froze for a moment, her tempered smile sharpening slightly. "Oh? And what did you have in mind exactly?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Well\'85" Johnny hesitated, already teetering too far forward to retreat. "I think it would do wonders if you delegated more responsibilities. Take a step back. Trust us to carry the heavier burdens of the research for a while! You could focus on\'97" He gestured vaguely to her abdomen. "You know."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Let me get this straight," Sarah said, crossing her arms and tilting her head just enough to imply danger. "You\'92d like me\'97who Eloise herself put in charge of overseeing the project\'97to gracefully vanish so you can\'85 what, Johnny? Take the lead?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Not take the lead. No, no, no. More like\'85" He seemed unable to verb his way out of trouble, his hands fluttering as though searching for an alibi. "Support from a position of\'85 um\'85 absent oversight?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah forced a tired smile and leaned forward slightly. "Johnny. Do you think I\'92m stupid?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That made him backpedal, both verbally and physically. "Sarah, of course not. Anyone who doesn\'92t appreciate your intellect is\'97well\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"A very specific brand of executive stress ball?" Sarah interrupted. She dropped the bag on the marble counter with a casual air that belied her irritation. "Let me save you the trouble, Johnny. I\'92m fine. And I don\'92t intend to step back. Not from Gyges, not from anything."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Of course! Of course!" Johnny sputtered, retreating toward the door like a defeated contestant on a reality show. "I just\'85 all I\'92m saying is, if you ever need someone to lighten the load, you can count on me."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah followed him to the door, her hands clasped thoughtfully in front of her. Something in her demeanor softened, catching Johnny off guard. "You know what, Johnny? I think you're right."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Oh!" His face lit up. "You do?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Yes. You\'92ve convinced me. I\'92ll take tonight off. Total rest while you fully step up and finalize the Phase Four configuration parameters. And, of course, you\'92ll draft Eloise a thorough report while you\'92re at it."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny blinked. "Phase Four... configuration?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah opened the door wide and ushered him out with a smile sweeter than poisoned honey. "Thank you, Johnny. You\'92re such a team player."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>By the time Johnny realized he\'92d just been assigned the most tedious and ambiguous task on the docket, he was halfway down the walkway. He turned back to protest, but Sarah had already shut the door. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Inside, she leaned against it and laughed softly to herself. She felt like a chess master playing someone who thought they held all the aces.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With a pang of regret, the realised that, alongside Johnny and Alexander and\'97come to think of it\'97most other men, who courted as if she was a prize-wining porker looking for a man\'92s pen to roll around in \'85 alongside all of them Tony was a the one true knight in somewhat tarnished armour.
